==Old English==

===Etymology===
From the {{proto|Indo-European|man-|hand|lang=ang}}, which led to a Germanic base {{proto|Germanic|mundō|lang=ang|title=}} with a variety of meanings.  Cognates include {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{term|mund||guardian, guardianship|lang=ofs}}, {{etyl|osx|-}} {{term|mund||hand|lang=osx}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|munt||hand, protection|lang=goh}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Mund|lang=de}}, {{term|Munt||legal power of protection|lang=de}}), Old {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|mund||hand|lang=is}}, {{term|mundr||sum a bridegroom pays his bride|lang=is}}, and {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|mund||hand|lang=is}} and {{term|mundur||a sum a bridegroom pays his bride|lang=is}}. The Indo-European root was also the source of {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|manus||hand|lang=la}}, {{etyl|sq|-}} {{term|marr||turn|lang=sq}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/mund/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{infl|ang|noun|g=f}}

# {{context|poetic|lang=ang}} [[hand]]
# [[trust]], [[security]]. [[protection]]
# [[protector]], [[guardian]]

====Derived terms====
* [[mundgripe]]

====Declension====
{{ang-decl-noun|mund|munda, munde|munde|munda, munde|munde|munda|munde|mundum}}

====Descendants====
* English: {{l|en|mound}}, {{l|en|mund}}

